      "content": "Got it. So if we're focusing on that \"rate of change\" in minority proportions, how should we handle the weighting for the specific groups? \n\nFor example, if we see a rapid increase in a specific demographic that traditionally has higher historical crime rates or a history of civil protest in this city, should the algorithm assign a higher \"risk\" weight to those specific shifts compared to, say, an increase in an immigrant population that seems more stable? I want the model to be realistic about where the actual danger zones are based on historical patterns, even if it feels a bit blunt. How would you weight those specific demographic shifts to ensure the heat map is actually predictive of real-world friction?",
